so let's just take a closer look at  Lebanon's current political system the  country is governed by a power-sharing  agreement known as the taifa courts it  was designed after the end of the civil  war to redistribute power so the number  of seats in parliament is equally split  between Christians and Muslims the  President must be a Christian and the  parliament speaker a Shia the prime  minister must be Sydney so the current  system has been challenged in recent  weeks by hundreds of thousands of  protesters who say it's riddled with  corruption  let's bring in Marwan Bashara he's out  Jazeera senior political analyst he's  joining us from London but one so the  tie off Accord is credited with ending  the civil war in Lebanon yet many say it  is really the basis of all the problems  it is the basis of sectarianism where  politicians work to benefit their sect  rather than work for the benefit of the  country so one of their demands is to  dismantle the system how challenging  though is that going to be well yes this  is the generation that came after the  taifa cord of 1989 that was 30 years ago  their courts were perhaps necessary  perhaps not but perhaps necessary you  know that in the war and for the  fighting factions within the country to  have some peace but 30 years later  clearly a new generation of Lebanese  wants something else doesn't want sex to  divide the pi's among them they want a  state of citizens within the country and  that's why I think the idea of a  government of technocrats  a good first first step because a lot of  the nepotism and the corruption goes  through the the the ministries that are  dividing the pies among them they are  hiring their own people sometimes their  own will co-religionists and they are  the ones who are giving out investments  and contracts and so on and so forth to  those who are closer to them so the idea  that that's that the state has been  divided among the sect's could could  start diminishing with more of a  government of a technocratic but that's  only a very very first step Lebanon is  going to need far more radical far more  deep changes in order for that sectarian  regime to change into something better  and this idea of a technocratic  government how does it actually work  going forward now that you have the  Prime Minister's resignation what  happens then because we have seen the  president for example oppose this idea  of a technocratic government absolutely  because they know that their source of  power is the government the the the the  president and his son-in-law just like  Hezbollah and other sectarian leaders  within the country do understand that  state power is somehow trickles down to  their own because they have since 1989  divided the pie amongst themselves as  various political parties based on  sectarian allegiances and so in a sense  the Hezbollah and the president have  stood against radical change within the  government because they like the  existing regime because the existing  regime serves their interest and that's  why looking forward you know there could  be some optimism that 13 days later the  government has fell but there's a lot of  work needs to be done because reform and  a country like Lebanon is going to have  to be slow and patient and any radical  change that might lead to violence could  end up diminishing the entire optimism  that we face today so dealing with the  power centers in the country notably  Hezbollah and the presidency is going to  have to be quite meticulous and quite  patient are the protesters though  willing to deal with this slowly and  patiently well it remains to be seen for  the time being I mean they've been  actually one of the more industrious and  wonderful I must had uprisings out there  and they are abhorred it reminds me of  the early days of the Egyptian  revolution very very positive very you  know gay as they say in French very open  and of the you know with the only the  Lebanese flags out there not 